Working with agents for the past months has convinced me that outcome-only evaluation is a flawed approach to benchmarking. It's essential to look at the logs to understand if the agent really did its job.

In our paper, we introduce a taxonomy of threats to credible evaluation of AI agents, outline four key principles for conducting log analysis effectively, present a case study of how log analysis helped us find various benchmarking errors on τ-bench, and provide recommendations to improve log analysis quality and adoption.

@PKirgis: "New paper: Log analysis is necessary for credible evaluation of AI agents. Benchmarks tell us what the agent achieved; only logs reveal how and why. As agents grow more capable and benchmarks more open-ended, that distinction will only matter more."

Log analysis is not a one-time technique; it requires constant effort in validating benchmark results. One reason it's hard to uncover evaluation bugs is that they only become apparent after models are capable enough to solve tasks or circumvent evaluation constraints.

On CORE-Bench, errors were not uncovered until Nicholas Carlini submitted the Claude Code agent, which saturated the benchmark accuracy. This highlights the need for recurring validation, as more capable models might find clever ways to exploit implementations. This mindset shift from viewing benchmarks as static is challenging but necessary for advancing evaluation science.

@sayashk: "I appreciate the work by @EpochAIResearch and @GregHBurnham in flagging and fixing these issues. Finding bugs in evaluations is always disappointing, but in the long run, it is necessary and extremely helpful for improving evaluations. As benchmarks become more complex, analyzing benchmark tasks and agent logs will become more important to ensure the validity of evaluation results."

